<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mayflower Compact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> Compact was an agreement signed by 41 men aboard the Mayflower off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a> on 11 November 1620 (Old Style), before the settlers went ashore. Lacking a valid patent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, they bound themselves into a 'civil body politic' to make just laws for the general good. It served as the basis of government in Plymouth Colony and is seen as an early American charter of self-government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mayflower-compact.jpg" alt="Mayflower Compact" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Leon Gerome Ferris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Mayflower_Compact_1620_cph.3g07155.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pilgrims had hoped to reach America in early October with two ships, but delays meant that only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> sailed. Their intended destination was the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, and the voyage was financed by the Company of Merchant Adventurers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Storms forced them to anchor at the hook of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-cod">Cape Cod</a> in Massachusetts, since provisions were running short. Some of the non-Puritan passengers, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> called Strangers, then declared that they would use their own liberty, because none had power to command them outside the agreed territory of Virginia.</p><p>To prevent this, the Pilgrims determined to establish their own government while affirming allegiance to the Crown. A similar argument had been made earlier by passengers of the Sea Venture, and Stephen Hopkins, thought to be the man of that name aboard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a>, had been convicted of mutiny and pardoned. The Pilgrims had lived for some years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. Nathaniel Philbrick says that, just as a spiritual covenant had marked the start of their congregation there, a civil covenant would provide the basis for a secular government in America.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The compact is a short text in which the signers, as loyal subjects of King James, state that they have undertaken a voyage to plant the first colony in the northern parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> for the glory of God, the advancement of the Christian faith and the honour of their king and country. They covenant and combine themselves into a civil body politic and promise to enact just and equal laws, ordinances and offices for the general good of the colony, to which they promise due submission and obedience.</p><p>The original document is lost. Three versions survive from the 17th century: in Mourt's Relation of 1622, in William Bradford's journal Of Plimoth Plantation of 1646, and in Nathaniel Morton's New-Englands Memorial of 1669. They differ slightly in wording and significantly in spelling and punctuation. Bradford's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is kept in a vault at the State Library of Massachusetts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Forty-one of the ship's 101 passengers signed. The list of signers, which includes John Carver, William Bradford, Myles Standish and John Alden, comes from Morton alone, and Thomas Prince first numbered the names in 1736. At a ceremony marking the 300th anniversary of the landing, on 23 November 1920, Massachusetts governor Calvin Coolidge called the compact the foundation of liberty based on law and order and the first real constitution of modern times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mayflower Compact” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Before landing, 41 men signed the <b>Mayflower Compact</b>, agreeing to form a civil body politic.</blockquote><a href="/state/plymouth-colony">Plymouth Colony</a></li></ul></section>
